course: dictionary
lesson: 8258
title: One word, three meanings: richly
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. When you meet one, check which meaning fits the sentence. «Richly» is a good example. It can describe something beautiful and expensive, something fully earned, or something with lots of colour or detail.
explain: Look at the words around it. «Joseph was given a richly decorated coat.» The word decorated tells you the coat was fancy and lovely to look at, so richly means in a beautiful, expensive or lavish way. Now try a different sentence: «The winner was richly rewarded.» Here richly means fully and well, so the reward was a full one.
explain: Try your meaning in the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have probably found the right one. If it does not, think of another meaning and try again. Context is your best clue.
fact: Richly is an adverb. It tells you how something is done or how something is.
fact: Richly can mean in a beautiful, expensive or lavish way, as in «richly dressed» or «richly decorated».
fact: Richly can mean fully and well, as in «richly deserved», which means completely earned.
fact: Richly can mean in large amounts or with strong colour, flavour or detail, as in «richly coloured» paintings.
fact: Words such as «ornamented» or «decorated» in the same sentence are clues that help you choose the right meaning.
exercise: «Jacob gave Joseph a richly decorated coat.» What does richly mean here? | Sadly, with tears | *In a beautiful, expensive or lavish way | In a small, plain way | Quickly and quietly
exercise: «The brave firefighter was richly rewarded for saving the children.» What does richly mean here? | *Fully and well, with a big reward | Slowly, after a long wait | With a small note on a list | Not at all, so there was no reward
exercise: Ella says a «richly coloured» painting is one with dull grey paint. Is she right? Which is the best answer? | Yes, grey is the only colour that counts as rich | No, richly coloured means using strong, deep colours | *No, richly coloured means using strong, deep colours. Grey is the opposite | Yes, because richly always means dull
test: Which word goes after richly to mean fully earned? «richly ___» | whispered | dropped | painted | *deserved
test: What does a «richly decorated» room look like? | *Full of fine details and lovely decorations | Empty and dusty after many years | Cold, dark and very quiet | Bare, with empty white walls and no furniture
exam: Tom says richly means having lots of money. Which meaning fits best in «richly coloured paintings»? | In a plain grey way | *With strong, deep colours | With lots of money | Very slowly and carefully
